60,3,1,talk,Misaki,,Candidate D has an engineering team present through rollout. That addresses my staffing fear.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Kenji,,Candidate D is built for transport operators. It should handle our yard devices and calendar.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Toshio,,Candidate B is a hosted suite with a fixed configuration. That might keep the costs stable.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Yumi,,Candidate B has a regional service team. That sounds like clear ownership. I like that.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,Candidate D is on our own servers. That means we control the upgrades. No skipped windows.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Kenji, D is on our servers. Does that mean we need more admins? We are losing two.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Misaki, it is built for us. It should be simpler to manage than the current mess.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Yumi, B is hosted. That means we pay a subscription. Is that cheaper than D's maintenance?","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Toshio, the subscription is predictable. D's maintenance might be cheaper, but the risk is higher.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Toshio, D's maintenance is for hardware we own. B's subscription is for their infrastructure.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Misaki,,Candidate C uses a partner firm for training. That is exactly what failed last time. I am against C.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"Candidate A is modular. That means separate licenses. Toshio, you said those climb yearly.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Kenji, correct. A is a trap. The add-ons will eat us alive. I am against A.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Yumi,,Candidate A scatters support across components. That is the winter outage all over again.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,Candidate C's marketplace is risky. We tried a trial tool and it was ringed with spreadsheets.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Misaki,,I vote for D. The on-site team is the only way to ensure the knowledge stays.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I vote for D. It is built for our yards. It will survive the peak season.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Toshio,,I vote for B. The fixed configuration and hosted model keep the costs predictable.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Yumi,,I vote for B. The regional team provides the clear ownership I need.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,I vote for D. On-premise gives us control over the upgrade cycle.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
